WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Dean from Dallas.&nbsp;
Dean from Dallas. Welcome to The David Pakman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.240
Show. What's on your mind today?
Speaker 2: Hey, David.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.160
I called in a couple of times. I don't know&nbsp;
if you remember me, but I wanted to ask a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.320
couple of questions. You know, there are these&nbsp;
buzzwords which are often associated with, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:25.680
a political party. Like when you hear patriotism&nbsp;
or freedom, you think of the right wing or MAGA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:32.700
Why doesn't the left attempt to take the positive&nbsp;
ones back? Why do we can see those terms that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.300
obviously apply to the party in big ways?
Speaker 1: We've talked about this before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.040
I think it's actually a great idea. Like when we&nbsp;
talk about what it means to be patriotic. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.040
a very cartoonish version on the right. And for&nbsp;
me, you know, patriotism would be, hey, you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:55.320
what? I take pride in my country making sure no&nbsp;
one is starving, that no one is homeless. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:01.620
like that would be a new form of patriotism that I&nbsp;
think would be much more interesting and inspiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:07.800
and positive. I can't answer the question why have&nbsp;
Democrats or the left not taken back these terms?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.520
There's a bunch of writing about this. George&nbsp;
Lakoff has talked about Republicans winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.340
the framing war. You know, I, I unfortunately,&nbsp;
Dean, I can't tell you why Democrats or the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.840
haven't done it. But you're absolutely right in&nbsp;
diagnosing that a lot of these terms have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.540
kind of perverted and certainly used exclusively&nbsp;
by the right to have narrow meanings and the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.020
should try to take them back. But I don't&nbsp;
really know what the first step would be.&nbsp;

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:37.680
Speaker 2: Yeah. And then I had a second question,&nbsp;
if you don't mind. Sure. Do you have any thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:44.280
on cultural assimilation? Do you think it's more&nbsp;
positive or negative, or does it make us stronger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.900
as a country or. What are your opinions on it?
Speaker 1: Well, I think that when people talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:56.880
about cultural assimilation, usually it's&nbsp;
often used to draw a very specific political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:03.540
perspective out of people. And my view on cultural&nbsp;
assimilation is, I think one of the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:10.020
makes the United States great is that you do have&nbsp;
all of these different cultures that when things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:17.580
are going well, can can sort of coexist. And I&nbsp;
think it's actually a great thing. And also, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:25.680
recognize that there are difficulties that surface&nbsp;
if you have different types of division, like when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.740
people talk about, hey, this is America, we speak&nbsp;
English here. Yet, listen, I mean, in a practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:39.060
sense, in order to best communicate, it's good to&nbsp;
have a shared language. So I agree with the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:43.320
that if someone comes to the United States and&nbsp;
lives in the United States for 30 years and speaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:50.880
no English, that's going to be an impediment both&nbsp;
for them and for the rest of society to relate. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:57.780
also think it's a great thing to understand and&nbsp;
respect and embrace different cultures, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.340
includes different languages and bilingualism&nbsp;
and all of these different things so that the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:09.240
you know, to say is cultural assimilation, good&nbsp;
or bad To some degree, if we're all going to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:15.780
together, we need to have some shared touch points&nbsp;
and language, respect for different religions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.580
respect for different countries of origins. All&nbsp;
of these different things are really important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:26.400
But to objectively just say cultural assimilation&nbsp;
is good or bad gets us into the kind of black and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.380
white thinking that often gets us nowhere.
Speaker 2: Yeah, it kind of seems like our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.180
country as a whole is just it's a constant&nbsp;
battle since we're so split of like what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:42.180
the new American dream or the American way of&nbsp;
living is like. It used to be like own a house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.640
you know, have kids married, get and get a good&nbsp;
job, go to college and all the things, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:53.220
like it's kind of shifting. And, you know, people&nbsp;
like right wing conservative players are saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.240
you know, college is where you go to get&nbsp;
work. Don't go to college or whatever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:05.160
It's. It's and you know, where people are&nbsp;
making more social protest. It's like during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:10.200
like the Pledge of Allegiance or, you know,&nbsp;
the singing of The Star-Spangled Banner or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.400
people not standing. You know, it's kind of. Yeah.
Speaker 1: And to be clear, for me, the Pledge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.500
Allegiance is just so silly. I don't even consider&nbsp;
it when I think about an issue like cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.999
assimilation. It's just nonsense to me.
Speaker 2: Okay.&nbsp;

00:04:21.999 --> 00:04:25.860
Speaker 1: You know. All right. Dean from Dallas,&nbsp;
appreciate the call. Great questions. Thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.360
All right. There goes Dean from Dallas.&nbsp;
Very, very powerful topics, I must say

